WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.750
The Marvel Cinematic Universe might look very
different had the God of Thunder not been

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:05.780
put through the wringer.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.000
Screenwriters Christopher Markus and Stephen
McFeely explained that they had to change

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.840
their plans due to the radical reinvention
of Thor in director Taika Waititi's ​Thor:

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:15.340
Ragnarok.​

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:20.500
Fans of the MCU know very well that Waititi
essentially rehabilitated the character of

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:25.320
Thor after his somewhat lackluster portrayal
in the series prior to Ragnarok.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.580
While there were always humorous undertones
to the God of Thunder, Waititi tapped into

00:00:29.599 --> 00:00:34.360
star Chris Hemsworth's comedic chops to give
us a Thor who was just as quippy as his fellow

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:35.360
Avengers.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:41.120
"But if I'm being honest, when it comes to
fighting evil beings, he is very powerful."

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:42.880
"Banner is powerful too."

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:44.060
"Is he, though?"

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.160
The hero's newly comedic personality felt
very different to his rather dour demeanor

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:54.330
in other MCU films, which meant that early
drafts of Infinity War needed to be adjusted

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:55.500
to accommodate it.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:01.980
"Remember, we were inheriting a Thor from
Ragnarok who was very well and radically re-toned

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:03.520
from the previous Avengers movies."

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:08.960
According to Markus and McFeely, they actually
had to fly in Hemsworth and Waititi to make

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:14.100
sure that the Odinson's characterization made
sense going from Thor: Ragnarok to Avengers:

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:14.980
Infinity War.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:19.420
But Waititi didn't just tweak the Thunder
God's characterization - he put the poor guy

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:23.380
through the kind of narrative gauntlet that
few Marvel characters have had to endure,

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.900
forcing him to experience more loss in a single
film than he ever had before.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:33.140
Markus and McFeely saw in this an opportunity
to portray Thor in a novel, almost shocking

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:33.700
way.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.960
In Avengers: Infinity War, the Thunder God
is obviously still hurting from the events

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:42.539
of Ragnarok, and in that film's opening, he's
forced to witness the murder of his adopted

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.440
brother Loki, as well as his friend Heimdall
at the hands of Thanos.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:52.200
During a later conversation with Rocket, Thor
seems withdrawn, unsure of himself, insecure,

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:53.180
even depressed.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.500
That continues to the end of the film, in
which Thor makes a crucial error, failing

00:01:57.509 --> 00:02:02.509
to immediately kill Thanos, allowing the Mad
Titan to snap away half of all life in the

00:02:02.509 --> 00:02:03.060
universe.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.840
The failure nearly breaks the Thunder God,
and when we see him in Avengers: Endgame,

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.380
he's despondent enough over it to kill a defenseless
Thanos in the film's opening moments.

00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:13.700
Markus explained:

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:19.060
"In Thor: Ragnarok, he loses his kingdom,
his father, his sister, and his eyeball.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:23.640
We just thought about what would happen if
any one of us sustained this loss and horror.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:26.560
You would get incredibly depressed and retreat
from the world.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:30.300
That is a comedic performance with a lot of
pain behind it."

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:34.489
If Waititi hadn't put Thor through the ringer
in Ragnarok, it's safe to say that Markus

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:38.340
and McFeely's interpretation of the character
would have been very different.

00:02:38.629 --> 00:02:42.959
Considering how instrumental Thor's failure
and subsequent breakdown are to the narrative

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:47.640
of Infinity War and Endgame, it's possible
that inheriting a steadier, more confident

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:52.300
Odinson might have changed the entire story
that Markus and McFeely were crafting.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:56.900
For that matter, the dour, kingly Thor of
his first two solo films almost certainly

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:00.750
wouldn't have made the choice he did at the
conclusion of Endgame to leave New Asgard

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:04.980
in the hands of Valkyrie and head out to go
on interstellar adventures with the Guardians

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:05.860
of the Galaxy.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:11.360
This development seems likely to affect the
MCU's overarching narrative for years to come;

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:15.829
writer/director James Gunn's upcoming Guardians
of the Galaxy Vol. 3 will certainly have to

00:03:15.829 --> 00:03:20.959
address it, and Thor's decision to leave Earth
behind is also certain to play into the story

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:23.120
of 2021's Thor: Love and Thunder.

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:27.880
The latter film will see Waititi return to
the franchise to showcase Thor's former flame

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:31.430
Jane Foster picking up the hammer to become
the Mighty Thor.

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:36.079
Indeed, it's easy to make the case that with
Thor: Ragnarok, Waititi didn't just reinvent

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:40.480
the Thor franchise - he shook up the entire
Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:45.180
It's also pretty amazing that Markus and McFeely
were able to incorporate the adjustments while

00:03:45.189 --> 00:03:50.599
also constructing the most complex narrative
the franchise has ever seen, and still crack

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:53.500
the story for the highest-grossing movie in
history.

